
This directory contains project tests.

Resident SELECT / automap rollback on opcode-23 startup failure (also in CI):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -IESP32/test/host_include -IESP32/include -Isrc \
  ESP32/test/script_startup_automap_host.c -Wl,--gc-sections \
  -o /tmp/doomrpg-script-startup-automap-test
/tmp/doomrpg-script-startup-automap-test
```

Includes the production resident owner directly: real SELECT routing, private
mode flags, closeAutomap and renderAutomapCurrent. Action/script startup, HUD,
visibility, raster/present and external gameplay owners are mocked. EXEC_FAILED
and INVALID restore the original automap/world context; unrelated mode bits
survive, failed restoration disables gameplay, failed close never starts the
script, and a successful modal pause does not restore automap prematurely.
Unselected modal/door/reward branches assert on invocation. This does not test
actual font/PAK failure, physical pixels, debit rollback or SD on CYD; the paid
interaction fixture separately covers the real script transaction.

Monster movement log budget / INFO versus TRACE (also in CI):

```sh
python3 ESP32/test/monster_log_policy_host.py
# Optional, estimate removed bytes from an existing Serial capture:
python3 ESP32/test/monster_log_policy_host.py --measure-log /path/to/capture.log
```

Compiles/preprocesses the six real movement/turn TUs at both levels, checks
16 detailed-success sites are absent at INFO, three conditional failure
witnesses remain, and attack-probe strings needed by the PC watcher survive.
Compiles/runs the real activation/capture gate with mocked lower owners at both
levels; success admits/captures identically while both failures still log.
No full AI, CYD timing or audio claim follows. The estimator reports UTF-8 byte
counts and ideal 115200/8N1 line time, not a measured gameplay speedup.

Credit-gated interaction, real compositor/gate + SHOW admission (also in CI):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -IESP32/test/host_include -IESP32/include -Isrc \
  ESP32/test/paid_interaction_host.c ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_event_chain.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_map_events.c ESP32/src/esp_map_event_filter.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_map_opcode_executor.c ESP32/src/esp_map_catalog.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_gib_fx.c ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_frame.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_native_dynamic_line_render.c ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_present_gate.c \
  -Wl,--gc-sections -Wl,--wrap=EspNativeGameplayFrame_renderTurn \
  -Wl,--wrap=EspNativeGameplayFrame_composeTurn -Wl,--wrap=EspMapRuntime_getLine \
  -Wl,--wrap=EspMapLineState_view -Wl,--wrap=Esp32PlatformVideo_present \
  -Wl,--wrap=EspNativeGameplayActionEngine_present \
  -o /tmp/doomrpg-paid-interaction-test
/tmp/doomrpg-paid-interaction-test
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -IESP32/test/host_include -IESP32/include ESP32/test/show_preview_host.c \
  -Wl,--gc-sections -o /tmp/doomrpg-show-preview-test
/tmp/doomrpg-show-preview-test
```

The first fixture replays the supplied event67 raw sequence through real event
filter/state/continuation, frame compositor, PresentGate, dynamic-line scope and
capture-painter code. Player/dialog/PAK/notebook/topology, raster children and
physical present are mocked; no SD or CYD claim follows. It retains the real
renderer rejection of pre-armed gates that caused the hardware WAIT stall;
composition publishes no incomplete world frame, effects bypass decorators,
open-line/lock-texture adaptation stays scoped, all cardinal angles complete.
The second
seeds the real compact topology storage and checks read-only SHOW admission,
unchanged successful commit/rollback, random blocker/linked/order/coordinate
guards. host_include is test-only SDK declaration glue, never a firmware input.

Dialog BACK touch / original cancel semantics (also in CI):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -IESP32/test/host_include -IESP32/include ESP32/test/dialog_touch_host.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_hub_touch_ui.c \
  -Wl,--gc-sections -o /tmp/doomrpg-dialog-touch-test
/tmp/doomrpg-dialog-touch-test
```

The real dialog owner/paint/input and shared crisp font are used; its private
state is seeded, and PAK/font-atlas blits/present are mocked. Every screen pixel
is classified: only the visible BACK card cancels EV_DIALOG without continuation;
EV_DIALOGNOBACK remains SELECT-only. Checks cover border/gap/OOB taps, lease
closure, no HUD overwrite and framebuffer guards. This adds no script YES/NO.

Architecture-consolidation audit tool (host Python, no firmware dependency):

```sh
python3 ESP32/test/elf_alloc_compare_host.py
python3 ESP32/tools/compare_elf_alloc.py ESP32/.pio/build/esp32-cyd/firmware.elf \
  --baseline /path/to/same-toolchain-base.elf \
  --forbid-prefix EspPostLoad --forbid-prefix EspNativePlayingService
```

Repeat with the bringup ELF and its own baseline. All non-empty SHF_ALLOC
sections must retain exact bytes/address/layout, including app metadata; NOBITS
uses layout. Debug-only differences are allowed, code/data/BSS changes are not.
CI tests the parser and checks retired exports in both final ELFs; the baseline
byte comparison is a separate controlled local check, not a hardware witness.

Level progress host regression (from the repository root):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -I ESP32/include \
  ESP32/test/test_level_progress.c ESP32/src/esp_player_fresh_map_state.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_map_catalog.c -o /tmp/test_level_progress
/tmp/test_level_progress
```

The production progress owner and map catalog are compiled unchanged; only the
player/HUD view and completion-checkpoint transport boundaries are mocked.
Tests cover fresh map entry, XP baseline
across a level-up, elapsed-time resume without offline/loading time, legacy-save
partial history, completion-byte roundtrip/owner rejection without replacing
progress, malformed map/complete flags, timer wrap and counter saturation.
The completion-mask validator itself is mocked, not validated by this fixture.

SYS direct-touch host regression (from the repository root):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -I ESP32/include \
  ESP32/test/test_sys_touch.c ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_save_touch.c \
  -o /tmp/test_sys_touch
/tmp/test_sys_touch
```

The production touch wrapper is compiled unchanged; its HUB cursor/navigation
and input boundaries are mocked. Checks cover every content pixel against the
shared three-card layout, rejection of gaps/margins, all nine cursor-to-target
routes, failed/invalid preselection, empty input and non-SYS fallback. While the
slot selector is open, checks cover its content rectangle and dispatch only
on touchSlot result 2, without navigating the underlying EXIT card. These
tests do not replace hardware confirmation of SAVE/LOAD or Exit To Menu.

Death-menu presentation / input host regression (from the repository root):

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
  -I ESP32/include -I src ESP32/test/test_death_menu.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_player_death.c \
  ESP32/src/esp_native_gameplay_hub_touch_ui.c \
  -Wl,--gc-sections -o /tmp/test_death_menu
/tmp/test_death_menu
# Optional: export the real painter at physical 320x240 for visual inspection.
/tmp/test_death_menu /tmp/death-with-save.ppm /tmp/death-no-save.ppm
```

Production death/menu code and the shared HUB font are compiled unchanged.
Clock, player/view/HUD, framebuffer/present and checkpoint boundaries are mocked.
Checks cover the fall and three-second menu admission, shared palette/text,
with/without save, card corners/gaps/margins, queued LOAD, selector open/back/
confirm, failed/unready selector admission, failed restore, successful selector
finish, unchanged deferred routes, framebuffer guards and failed presentation.
The selector is mocked: its actual pagination/storage code is not exercised.
These three fixtures are now required by the CYD CI workflow. This does not validate
actual SD restore, combat or display behavior on the CYD.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
